    "headline": "In November 2025, Inception raised $50 million to build diffusion language models. Instead of writing one token at a time, they refine a whole answer at once, and were benchmarked at over 1,000 tokens per second.",
    "kicker": "Apply day 7: can writing in parallel scale like reading did?",
    "fullText": "Most chatbots, including GPT-5 and Gemini, are autoregressive: they predict one word or word fragment at a time, in order. Inception, led by Stanford professor Stefano Ermon, builds diffusion models for text and code instead. They refine the whole output in steps until it reaches the target, which lets them run many operations at once.\n\nMenlo Ventures led the $50 million seed round, with Microsoft's M12, Nvidia's NVentures, Snowflake and Databricks taking part, plus Andrew Ng and Andrej Karpathy. Ermon credits the parallel design for benchmarks of over 1,000 tokens per second.\n\nThis is the day 7 pattern again. The Transformer won by dropping recurrence so it could process a sequence in parallel. Diffusion is a bet that writing in parallel can scale too.",
